 Yes, you can set up a validation rule in Jira to prevent an issue from transitioning to the next column if all items on a checklist are not marked as "Done." Here's how:

1. Go to your project in Jira and navigate to the issue type screen (Project settings > Issues > Issue types).
2. Find the issue type that matches your use case or create a new one if necessary.
3. Click on the "Validators" tab and add the following validator: "Script Approval Validator".
4. Write a Groovy script to validate if all checklist items are marked as "Done". Here's an example script:

```groovy
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.*
import com.atlassian.jira.component.ComponentAccessor

def component = ComponentAccessor.getIssueComponent()
def checklists = issue.getCustomfieldvalues('customfield_10005') // replace with the ID of your custom field for checklists
def allItemsDone = true

for (checklist in checklists) {
    def checklistItems = checklist.getValue().toString().split("\n")
    for (item in checklistItems) {
        if (!item.startsWith("Done:")) {
            allItemsDone = false
            break
        }
    }
    if (!allItemsDone) {
        break
    }
}

if (!allItemsDone) {
    throw new ValidateException("All checklist items must be marked as 'Done' before this transition is allowed.")
}
```

Replace `customfield_10005` with the ID of the custom field used to store the checklists. This script checks if each line in the checklist string starts with "Done:", indicating that an item is completed. If any item is not marked as done, the transition will be blocked.

Make sure to save the changes and test the validation rule thoroughly. Remember that Jira might need to be restarted after creating or updating the validator script.

