 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function to smooth the image and then apply the Sobel operator as you have already done. Here's how you can modify your code:
```MATLAB
% Read input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Use a standard deviation of 3 for the Gaussian filter
% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x direction on the filtered image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator in y direction on the filtered image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display the original image and the edge image
subplot(1,2,1), imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1,2,2), imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```
This modified code applies a Gaussian filter using `imgaussfilt` with a standard deviation of 3 pixels before performing edge detection using the Sobel operator. This helps to reduce the noise in the image and improve the accuracy of the edge detection results.

