 To retrieve the username from a JSON Web Token (JWT) in an ASP.NET C# WebAPI project, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` object and its `FindFirst()` method to get the specific claim containing the username. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. In your controller or action method, add the following using statements at the top of your file:

```csharp
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
```

2. Create a helper method to extract the username from the JWT token:

```csharp
private string GetUsernameFromToken(string token)
{
    var handler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();

    SecurityToken securityToken;
    ClaimsPrincipal principal = handler.ValidateToken(token, new TokenValidationParameters(), out securityToken);

    return principal.FindFirst(x => x.Type == ClaimTypes.Name).Value;
}
```

3. Use the helper method to retrieve the username within your action method:

```csharp
[Authorize] // Assuming your API is protected by JWT authentication
public IActionResult GetUserData([FromHeader(Name = "Authorization")] string authHeader)
{
    string token = authHeader.Replace("Bearer ", ""); // Remove "Bearer " prefix

    string username = GetUsernameFromToken(token);

    // Perform any further processing with the retrieved username
    ...
}
```

