response:
 In Rust, you can use match statements instead of multiple if-else conditions for this problem. Here's how you can write the `is_even` function in Rust:

```rust
fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {
 match n {
 0 => true,
 1 | 3 | 5 | 7 | 9 => false,
 _ => {
 if n % 2 == 0 {
 true
 } else {
 false
 }
 }
 }
}
```

This function takes an integer as an argument and returns a boolean value indicating whether it is even or odd. The `match` statement checks if the number is one of the specified values (0, 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9). If it is, the corresponding branch is executed, returning the appropriate result. If the number does not match any of the given cases, the remaining branch is executed, which calculates whether the number is even by checking if its remainder when divided by 2 is equal to zero.

