 I'm here to help you write a React utility function to remove widows in text. Widows are the last line of a paragraph that contains only one or a few letters, which can look visually unpleasant. Here's a simple and effective solution using the `react-document-renderer` library:

First, make sure to install the required dependencies:

```bash
npm install react-document react-document-renderer
```

Next, create a new file called `removeWidows.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { Document, Paragraph, Text } from 'react-document';

const removeWidows = (text) => {
  const lines = text.split('\n');
  const adjustedLines = lines.map((line, index) => {
    const words = line.trim().split(' ');
    if (words[words.length - 1].match(/^[.?!]$/)) {
      // If the last word is a period, question mark, or exclamation mark, add an extra space before it to prevent widows
      words[words.length - 1] += ' ';
    }
    return words.join(' ') + ' ';
  });

  const adjustedText = adjustments => adjustments.reduce((acc, adjustment) => acc + adjustment, '') + '\n';

  return adjustedText(lines.map(adjustedLine => (
    <Paragraph key={index}>
      <Text text={adjustedLine} />
    </Paragraph>
  )));
};

export default removeWidows;
```

